TITLE:: SuperDirtGUI
summary:: extra synths, effects, sample players, a mixer window and a sample editor for SuperDirt, loaded into a running instance
categories:: Libraries>SuperDirtGUI
related:: Classes/SDGWindow, Classes/SDGInsert, Classes/SDGSession, Classes/SDGSampleEditor, Classes/SDGSamplePlayer, Classes/SuperDirt, Classes/DirtOrbit, Classes/GlobalDirtEffect, Classes/DirtModule

DESCRIPTION::
A loader that reads four folders of plain code::.scd:: files - code::synths/::,
code::modules/::, code::players/:: and code::effects/:: next to its own
code::classes/:: folder - into a link::Classes/SuperDirt:: you already run, and
owns what the window and the sample editor need on top: a fader per orbit, the
sample-player module, and the library helpers. SuperDirt itself is never
changed, so this lives next to a normal install and updates on its own.

code::
s.boot;
~dirt = SuperDirt(2, s);
~dirt.loadSoundFiles;
~dirt.start(57120, 0 ! 12);
~sdg = SuperDirtGUI(~dirt);
~sdg.gui;
::

Every file is loaded the way SuperDirt loads its own code::synths/:: folder,
with code::~dirt:: bound to the instance and code::~sdg:: to this loader, in
alphabetical order. Each folder ships one example file that is a template:
link::#-newFile:: - new... in the window's modules tab - copies it under a new
name, or copy it by hand.

table::
## strong::folder:: || strong::one file holds:: || strong::switched on from Tidal by::
## code::synths/:: || a SynthDef in SuperDirt's argument convention || code::s "sdgsine"::
## code::modules/:: || code::~dirt.addModule(name, func, test):: and its SynthDef, code::"name" ++ ~dirt.numChannels:: || the parameter its test looks for
## code::players/:: || code::~sdg.addSamplePlayer(name, synthFunc, test)::, another way of reading a sample - see link::Classes/SDGSamplePlayer:: || the parameter its test looks for
## code::effects/:: || code::SynthDef("global_name" ++ ~dirt.numChannels):: and code::~sdg.addGlobalEffect(\global_name, [\params]):: || one of its parameters
::

subsection:: Who owns what

Synths are added to link::Classes/SynthDescLib::.global and sent to the
server; they stay there after link::#-free::, since Tidal may still be playing
them. Modules are added to code::~dirt.modules:: and removed by
link::#-free:: and link::#-reload::. A file that redefines one of SuperDirt's
own modules - code::lpf::, say - replaces it, but the module is not counted as
ours, so link::#-free:: never takes SuperDirt's away. Global effects are one
link::Classes/GlobalDirtEffect:: per orbit, put first in the orbit's chain,
ahead of SuperDirt's delay and reverb; link::#-free:: releases them and hands
the orbits back the way they were.

Two things are added by the class itself, not by a file. A module named
code::sdgplayer:: sits in front of SuperDirt's own code::sound:: module: when a
sample event asks for one of the players it sends that player's synth with the
arguments SuperDirt would have sent, and sets code::~diversion:: - the key
SuperDirt's own module checks before playing - so the sample is heard once. And
a global effect named code::global_sdgfader:: is put in every orbit last before
SuperDirt's code::dirt_rms:: and code::dirt_monitor::. It scales the orbit's
dry and effect busses in place by its one parameter, code::orbitAmp::, which is
what link::#-setFader::, link::#-setMute:: and link::#-setSolo:: set. The
monitor after it is SuperDirt's, untouched. Both come out again with
link::#-free::.

Orbits exist only after code::~dirt.start::, so make the loader after
code::start::. Made before, it loads synths, modules and players at once and
adds the global effects on its own once the orbits appear. After a later
code::~dirt.stop; ~dirt.start(...):: call link::#-applyGlobalEffects::
yourself: new orbits do not know about us.

subsection:: Files, one at a time

The loader remembers every file it read and what the file defined, so one file
can be read again on its own: link::#-loadFile::, which the window calls within
a second of a file being saved (link::#-reloadChangedFiles::). What the file no
longer defines is taken out - its modules and players come out, its global
effects leave every orbit - unless another file defines it too. A global effect
it still defines starts again on its new SynthDef and parameters, in its place
in each orbit's chain, and the other effects of the chain keep running, tails
and all; one it defines for the first time goes first into every orbit. An
orbit the effect was taken out of in the window does not get it back.

link::#-newFile:: makes a new file from one that is there and loads it - the
window's new... button - after link::#-nameProblem:: has made sure the name
means nothing yet. link::#-sourceFile:: and link::#-entries:: say where each
name came from.

subsection:: Inserts

Every orbit can have inserts, the way a channel of a desk does: an eq, a
compressor, an insight or a VST plugin - the ParaEQ, Compressor, Insight and
VSTPlugin extensions, each looked up by name and offered only when installed -
after SuperDirt's delay,
reverb and leslie and before the fader, in an order you choose, as many as you
like. Each is a global effect of SuperDirt's own kind, so SuperDirt keeps it in
place through every rebuild of the orbit; link::Classes/SDGInsert:: says how.

code::
i = ~sdg.addInsert(0, \eq);
c = ~sdg.addInsert(0, \compressor);
~sdg.moveInsert(c, 0);
~sdg.insertsOf(0);
~sdg.removeInsert(i);
::

link::#-reload:: keeps them, with their settings; link::#-free:: takes them out.

subsection:: Tags, and picking samples by them

link::#-tags:: is the library's analysis: every sample's tags, bpm and key, set
by hand where you set them (link::Classes/SDGSampleTags::). The loader also puts
a picker in every orbit's default event, as its code::diversion:: - what
SuperDirt calls before it looks the sound up. An event with code::sdgtag::,
code::sdgnot::, code::sdgbpm:: or code::sdgkey:: gets its code::s:: and code::n::
replaced by a sample that matches: at random, or the n-th match when the event
has an code::n::. code::s:: names a bank to pick inside, or
link::#*anySoundName:: (code::sdgany::) for the whole library. A diversion that
was there before is called after it, and link::#-free:: puts it back.

code::
~sdg.tags.analyseLibrary;
(type: \dirt, dirt: ~dirt, s: \sdgany, sdgtag: "percussive.short", sdgnot: \melodic).play;
::

From Tidal: code::d1 $ s "sdgany*8" # pS "sdgtag" "percussive.short" # pS "sdgnot" "melodic"::.

subsection:: Notifications

The loader is a model for the window: it sends code::changed:: with
code::\loaded:: (after load, reload and free, with no more arguments; after one
file was read, with its kind and path), code::\samples:: (a bank or a file was
added, the bank name when there is one), code::\reloaded:: (name and index of a
sample re-read after a save), code::\mixer:: (a fader, mute or solo moved) and
code::\inserts:: (an orbit's inserts changed, with its index). Anything can
depend on it the usual way.

subsection:: Files that fail

A file that does not compile is reported with the parse error and a warning; a
file that throws, with the error. The other files still load. A file read again
on its own that fails leaves in place what it loaded before - so a save with a
typo in it never silences what was playing - and is marked failed until a save
that loads: link::#-fileFailed::, and in the window's list. link::#-reload::
takes everything out first, so there a failing file loses what it had.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: useMaster
Whether a new SuperDirtGUI makes a master and sends its orbits through it. true by default.
ARGUMENT:: bool
true or false, set before code::SuperDirtGUI(~dirt)::.


METHOD:: loadOwnSamples
Whether a new SuperDirtGUI loads the six banks rendered from its drum voices
(code::samples/::, see link::#-loadSamples::) into the sound library. true by
default; a second loader finds them there and says nothing.
ARGUMENT:: bool
true or false, set before code::SuperDirtGUI(~dirt)::.

METHOD:: new
Make the loader and load everything: the four content folders, then the rendered
banks (link::#*loadOwnSamples::).
ARGUMENT:: dirt
the link::Classes/SuperDirt:: to load into, usually code::~dirt::.
RETURNS:: a SuperDirtGUI.

METHOD:: current
The loader made last, or nil after it was freed. link::Classes/SDGWindow:: and
link::Classes/SDGSampleEditor:: fall back to it.

METHOD:: registerBusses
Name every orbit's code::dryBus:: and code::globalEffectBus:: in the
BusManager registry as code::orbit0_dry::, code::orbit0_fx:: and so on, so they can be
metered and listened to from its window. A no-op when that quark is not
installed; safe to call twice. The window calls it when it opens.
ARGUMENT:: dirt
a started link::Classes/SuperDirt::; code::SuperDirt.default:: or the current
loader's when nil.

METHOD:: plumbingEffectNames
SuperDirt's own orbit effects, code::[\dirt_rms, \dirt_monitor]::, which the
fader is kept in front of and the window never lists.

METHOD:: faderEffectName
The name of the fader effect, code::\global_sdgfader::.

METHOD:: anySoundName
The sound that means the whole library to the tag picker; code::\sdgany::.

METHOD:: splitTags
ARGUMENT:: value
code::"percussive.short"::, a Symbol, or an Array.
RETURNS:: an Array of Symbols: tags joined with a dot, as Tidal's words allow.

METHOD:: playerModuleName
The name of the sample-player module, code::\sdgplayer::.

METHOD:: kinds
The four kinds of content, code::[\synths, \modules, \players, \effects]::,
each read from the folder of the same name.

METHOD:: fileNotes
What a content file says about itself, from the comments above its code. The
description is the first paragraph, without its code::folder/file.scd - ::. The
parameters are the lines under code::// parameters:::, one each, with two spaces
or more between the columns: name, range, what it does.
code::
//   sdgcut     30 to 18000 Hz       the filter with its envelope closed
::
Comments, so a file that does not compile still has them, and new... copies them
along. Nothing checks a range: it says what is useful, the SynthDef clips what it
clips.
ARGUMENT:: filePath
the file's path; nil or a missing file answers empty notes.
RETURNS:: an Event, code::(description:, params: [(name:, range:, text:)], listed:)::,
with code::listed:: true when the file has the section.

METHOD:: kindLabel
What one of a kind is called in messages and in the window: code::"player"::
for code::\players::, code::"global effect":: for code::\effects::.

METHOD:: baseName
A name without code::global_:: for a global effect - its parameter - and as it
is for the other kinds. Answers a String.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.
ARGUMENT:: name
a String or Symbol.

METHOD:: fullName
The name a kind registers under, from the name or, for a global effect, its
parameter: code::SuperDirtGUI.fullName(\effects, \sdgspace):: is
code::\global_sdgspace::. Answers a Symbol.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.
ARGUMENT:: name
a String or Symbol.

METHOD:: eventWords
The event keys SuperDirt, Tidal or the player module read without any SynthDef
or default event declaring them - the core and internal part of SuperDirt's own
code::used-parameters.scd::, and code::player:: and code::diversion::. Part of
what link::#-nameProblem:: refuses.

METHOD:: samePath
Whether two paths name the same place however they are spelled: code::~::
expanded, a backslash read as a slash, no trailing separator, and on Windows no
case. Every comparison of paths here goes through it - on Windows a path made
with code::+/+:: has backslashes and one from elsewhere may have slashes.
ARGUMENT:: a
a path.
ARGUMENT:: b
another.


METHOD:: bankName
The bank a folder makes: its name, with what Tidal's mini notation would read as
something else, spaces above all, as code::_::. code::"~/my kit/":: is code::\my_kit::.
ARGUMENT:: folderPath
a String.
RETURNS:: a Symbol.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: recorder
Open the BusRecorder window. When the recorder holds no sources, or only orbit
sources left from an earlier SuperDirt, it gets the preset first
(link::#-recordOrbits::); any other list is left alone, as is everything during
a take. A warning and nil when BusRecorder is not installed or there are no
orbits yet.
RETURNS:: the BusRecorderGUI, or nil.

METHOD:: recordOrbits
The recorder's preset: replaces its sources with one per orbit, named
code::orbit0::, code::orbit1:: and so on, reading link::#-tapBusOf::, with one
file per source, labelled by name, in 32-bit float. The take name and the folder
stay as they are. Refused with a warning during a take.
RETURNS:: the BusRecorder class, or nil.

METHOD:: tapBusOf
The bus holding an orbit's sound after its effects, inserts, fader, mute and
solo, what its meters read: dry and effect busses summed by the fader effect,
with as many channels as SuperDirt has. The monitor's limiter is not in it. It is silent
while the orbit sleeps. The bus belongs to the orbit: code::reload:: and a
second loader on the same SuperDirt keep it, code::free:: frees it and takes the
sources reading it out of the recorder.
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
the orbit, from 0.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Bus::, or nil while the orbit has no fader.

METHOD:: load
Read all four folders. Called by code::new::.

METHOD:: reload
Take our modules, players and global effects out, then read every file again.
Edited a file? This is what to call - no reboot, no recompile.

METHOD:: loadSynths
METHOD:: loadModules
METHOD:: loadSamplePlayers
METHOD:: loadGlobalEffects
One folder each.

METHOD:: loadFile
Read one file of the four folders again, or for the first time, as the
subsection on files one at a time above says. Edited a file with the window
closed? This reads just that file.
ARGUMENT:: filePath
absolute, or relative to the repository: code::"players/mywarp.scd"::.
RETURNS:: true when the file ran; false when it did not compile, threw, or is
gone (and what it defined was taken out).

METHOD:: reloadChangedFiles
Every file of the four folders whose text is not what was read last - edited,
new, or gone - through link::#-loadFile::. The window calls it once a second
while it is open.
RETURNS:: the paths it read, an Array.

METHOD:: synthNames
METHOD:: moduleNames
METHOD:: samplePlayerNames
METHOD:: globalEffectNames
What the four folders added, as Symbols, in load order.

METHOD:: namesOf
One of the four, by kind.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.

METHOD:: sourceFile
The file a name came from.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.
ARGUMENT:: name
the name; a global effect by its name or by its parameter, code::\global_sdgspace::
or code::\sdgspace::.
RETURNS:: the path, or nil for a name registered from code rather than a file.

METHOD:: presetsOf
A synth's presets, which Tidal's code::n:: picks: its events in SuperDirt's sound library, as
its file registered them or as link::Classes/SDGSynthPresets:: replaced them. Each names itself
in code::presetName::. A synth with none answers an empty Array, and its code::n:: is its pitch.
ARGUMENT:: synth
the synth's name.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/Event::s

METHOD:: presetNames
The names of link::#-presetsOf::, by n; code::"preset 3":: for one without.
ARGUMENT:: synth
the synth's name.

METHOD:: presetValues
What preset n sets of the synth's controls - not code::out::, code::freq::, code::sustain::,
code::pan:: or code::accelerate::, which every event sets itself.
ARGUMENT:: synth
the synth's name.
ARGUMENT:: n
the preset.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::

METHOD:: savePreset
Add a preset at the end of a synth's list, under a name, and play it from now on: the whole list
is written by link::Classes/SDGSynthPresets:: and put into the sound library. Notifies
code::\presets:: with the synth's name.
code::
n = ~sdg.savePreset(\sdgkick, "short boom", (sdgdecay: 0.4, sdgclick: 0.1));
// d1 $ s "sdgkick:10"
::
ARGUMENT:: synth
the synth's name.
ARGUMENT:: name
what the window's menu calls it.
ARGUMENT:: values
an Event of the synth's controls.
RETURNS:: its n.

METHOD:: removePreset
Take preset n out of a synth's list; every one after it moves down by one. Kept on this machine
as link::#-savePreset:: is. Notifies code::\presets::.
ARGUMENT:: synth
the synth's name.
ARGUMENT:: n
the preset.
RETURNS:: false when there is no preset n.

METHOD:: restoreFilePresets
Forget this machine's list of a synth and play the presets its file registers again. Notifies
code::\presets::.
ARGUMENT:: synth
the synth's name.

METHOD:: hasOwnPresets
Whether a synth plays this machine's list rather than its file's.
ARGUMENT:: synth
the synth's name.

METHOD:: entries
What the window's modules tab lists for a kind: an Event per name,
code::(name:, path:, failed:)::, with code::path:: nil for a name registered
from code; then one per file that failed before it defined anything, with
code::name:: nil.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.

METHOD:: notes
What a name does and takes: link::#*fileNotes:: of its file. A file without a
parameters section, or a name registered from code, gets the controls of its
SynthDef instead, each code::(name:, range: nil, default:, text: ""):: - without
the ones every synth of its kind has, such as code::out:: or code::sustain::.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.
ARGUMENT:: name
a name of that kind.
RETURNS:: an Event, as for link::#*fileNotes::.

METHOD:: fileFailed
Whether a file's last read failed - it did not compile, or threw - so that what
plays is what it loaded before, if anything.
ARGUMENT:: filePath
absolute, or relative to the repository.

METHOD:: templateName
The name a folder's example file defines: what link::#-newFile:: copies when
it is not told what to copy. The first name that came from a file of the folder
when there is no example file.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.

METHOD:: nameProblem
Why a name will not do for a new file of a kind, or nil when it will. The name
becomes an argument name and an event key, so it must be an identifier: a
lowercase letter, then letters, digits or code::_::. And it must mean nothing
yet: no file of that name in the folder, none of our names, no module of
SuperDirt's, no sample bank or sound of the library, no key of the orbits'
default events, no parameter of their effects, no SynthDef and no parameter of
one, none of link::#*eventWords::, and none of the SynthDefs the new file would
build. A module named code::speed:: would switch on with every event that has a
speed; one named code::lpf:: would replace SuperDirt's own.
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.
ARGUMENT:: name
a String or Symbol; a global effect by its parameter, with or without
code::global_::.
RETURNS:: a String saying what the word already is, or nil.

METHOD:: newFile
Make a file from one that is there and load it. The source's text has the
source's name - and its file name - replaced by the new one wherever it stands
as a word: a letter or digit next to it makes it part of another word, a
code::_:: does not, so code::global_sdgspace:: and code::sdgwarp_1_2:: change
with it and code::sdgspacer:: does not. Its other parameters keep their names.
code::
~sdg.newFile(\players, \mywarp);            // players/mywarp.scd from the example
~sdg.newFile(\effects, \myecho, \sdgspace);   // effects/myecho.scd from sdgspace's file
::
ARGUMENT:: kind
one of link::#*kinds::.
ARGUMENT:: name
the new name, which link::#-nameProblem:: must find nothing wrong with. A
global effect by its parameter; code::global_:: is added.
ARGUMENT:: from
a name of the same kind whose file is copied; link::#-templateName:: when nil.
RETURNS:: the path of the new file, or nil, with a warning, when the name will
not do or there is no file to copy.

METHOD:: addSamplePlayer
Called from a file in code::players/::. Registers a link::Classes/SDGSamplePlayer::,
builds its SynthDefs, puts it at the front of the list (the most recently added
player wins when several tests pass), and installs the player module the first
time. Re-adding a name replaces it in place.
ARGUMENT:: name
the Tidal parameter that switches it on, e.g. code::\sdgwarp::.
ARGUMENT:: synthFunc
a function of code::(sampleNumChannels, numChannels):: answering the SynthDef
function - see link::Classes/SDGSamplePlayer:: for the argument convention.
ARGUMENT:: test
evaluated inside the event, e.g. code::{ ~sdgwarp.notNil }::.
ARGUMENT:: defPrefix
the SynthDef names are code::<defPrefix>_<sampleChannels>_<numChannels>::;
the name itself by default.
ARGUMENT:: rates
the control rates of the nine standard controls; all code::\ir:: but
code::pan:: by default.
ARGUMENT:: prepare
optional, run inside the event just before its arguments are read - see
link::Classes/SDGSamplePlayer#Prepare::.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGSamplePlayer::.

METHOD:: removeSamplePlayer
Take a player out; the module goes with the last one.

METHOD:: getSamplePlayer
The player of that name, or nil.

METHOD:: samplePlayerFor
The player for a sample event: an explicit code::~player:: wins, otherwise the
first whose test passes; nil when none wants it, and SuperDirt plays the sample
as always.
ARGUMENT:: event
the event, with the sound event merged in.

METHOD:: samplePlayers
The registered players, in the order they are asked.

METHOD:: addGlobalEffect
Called from a file in code::effects/::. Registers a SynthDef name and the event
keys it listens to; link::#-applyGlobalEffects:: then gives every orbit an
instance.
ARGUMENT:: name
the SynthDef name without the channel count, e.g. code::\global_sdgspace::.
ARGUMENT:: paramNames
an Array of Symbols: the Tidal parameters that wake the effect and are passed on.
ARGUMENT:: alwaysRun
true to keep it awake even when no parameter arrives. false by default.

METHOD:: offerGlobalEffect
Called from a file in code::effects/::, in place of link::#-addGlobalEffect::, for an
effect no orbit gets by itself. It waits in the add menu of the window's effects tab
and costs nothing until link::#-addTrack:: puts it
somewhere. A reload keeps it where it was added and puts it nowhere else. It always
runs, since an event need not carry any of its parameters, so its SynthDef's defaults
should make a sound.
ARGUMENT:: name
the SynthDef name without the channel count, e.g. code::\global_sdghole::.
ARGUMENT:: paramNames
an Array of Symbols: the event keys that are passed on.
ARGUMENT:: specs
optional, an Event of parameter -> what code::asSpec:: takes, the ranges of the
window's knobs: code::(sdgholetime: [0.05, 2, \exp])::.

METHOD:: offeredEffectNames
RETURNS:: the names registered with link::#-offerGlobalEffect::.

METHOD:: effectParamSpec
The knob range an effect's file gave a parameter.
ARGUMENT:: name
the effect's name, code::\global_sdghole::.
ARGUMENT:: param
the parameter.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/ControlSpec::, or nil.

METHOD:: applyGlobalEffects
Give every orbit its own link::Classes/GlobalDirtEffect:: for each registered
name, first in the chain, and the fader last before SuperDirt's rms and
monitor, then rebuild the node tree and push the mixer's state onto the new
fader synths. An orbit that already has one is left alone, so calling it twice
changes nothing. Waits for the server once, since code::SynthDef.add:: is
asynchronous.

METHOD:: globalEffectSpecs
The registered specs, one Event per effect: code::name::, code::paramNames::,
code::alwaysRun::.

METHOD:: saveSession
The whole mixer under a name, over any of that name; link::Classes/SDGSession:: says
what that holds. It takes a moment: every plugin is asked for its state.
ARGUMENT:: name
The session's name.
ARGUMENT:: action
Called with true, or false with a warning.

METHOD:: loadSession
A saved mixer back, in place of what the orbits hold now.
ARGUMENT:: name
The session's name.
ARGUMENT:: action
Called with true once everything is playing, or false with a warning, and then
nothing was touched.

METHOD:: sessionNames
RETURNS:: the saved sessions, an Array of link::Classes/String::.

METHOD:: sessionName
RETURNS:: the session saved or loaded last, nil before the first.

METHOD:: session
RETURNS:: this loader's link::Classes/SDGSession::.

METHOD:: controlWindow
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGWindow:: while it is open, nil otherwise.

METHOD:: insertsOf
The inserts of an orbit, in the order the signal goes through them.
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
0 for the first orbit.
RETURNS:: an Array of link::Classes/SDGInsert::.

METHOD:: addInsert
An insert on an orbit: into its chain before the fader, its synth started in
its place and nothing else restarted.
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
0 for the first orbit.
ARGUMENT:: kind
code::\eq::, code::\compressor::, code::\insight:: or code::\vst::.
ARGUMENT:: index
its position among the orbit's inserts; the end when nil.
ARGUMENT:: plugin
for code::\vst::, the plugin: a name VSTPlugin's search found (see
link::#-vstPlugins::) or a file path.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGInsert::, or nil when there is no such orbit,
the kind's extension is not installed, or a VST insert has no plugin.

METHOD:: vstPlugins
The VST effects the server can open, by VSTPlugin's own search - quick for
plugins in its cache, slow the first time it meets many (a minute is not
unusual).
ARGUMENT:: action
called with their VSTPluginDescs, instruments left out, sorted by name.
ARGUMENT:: rescan
true to search again, for plugins installed since.

METHOD:: orderInserts
Walk an orbit's inserts into the order of its list on the server: moved, never
started again. Every rebuild here calls it; it is what puts a VST insert, which
SuperDirt does not know about, back in its place.
ARGUMENT:: orbit
a link::Classes/DirtOrbit::.

METHOD:: setOutBus
Send an orbit straight to a hardware bus, past the master: the same as
code::setOrbitDest(i, \direct, bus)::.
ARGUMENT:: i
the orbit's index.
ARGUMENT:: bus
a bus index or a Bus.

METHOD:: setOrbitDest
Where an orbit goes: the master, or straight to a hardware bus. Its monitor's
output bus changes - code::DirtOrbit:outBus_::, which rebuilds the orbit, with its
effects released so that sleeping ones are freed and its inserts put back in place -
only when the bus it comes to changes. The window's output menu uses it.
ARGUMENT:: i
the orbit's index.
ARGUMENT:: dest
code::\master:: or code::\direct::.
ARGUMENT:: bus
for code::\direct::, the hardware bus; nil keeps the one it had.

METHOD:: orbitDest
ARGUMENT:: i
the orbit's index.
RETURNS:: where it goes: code::\master:: or code::\direct::.

METHOD:: directBusOf
ARGUMENT:: i
the orbit's index.
RETURNS:: the hardware bus it writes to when it goes direct.

METHOD:: hardwareOut
RETURNS:: where orbit 0 wrote when the add-on first saw it: where the master writes, and every direct route starts.

METHOD:: moveInsert
An insert to another position among its orbit's inserts. The synths are moved,
not started again.
ARGUMENT:: insert
an link::Classes/SDGInsert::.
ARGUMENT:: toIndex
its new position.

METHOD:: removeInsert
Take an insert out: its synth released, which fades the orbit's busses back,
its object, window and insight freed.
ARGUMENT:: insert
an link::Classes/SDGInsert::.

METHOD:: removeInserts
Every insert of an orbit out.
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
0 for the first orbit.

METHOD:: startMeters
Switch on SuperDirt's rms replies, the ones the window's meters read, at a
rate that lands. code::DirtOrbit:startSendRMS:: hands the rate to the effect
while it is still switched off, so it never arrives and the replies come 8 a
second with peaks held 3 seconds; this puts the rate into the orbit's default
event and the effect's state first and rebuilds the orbit. The window calls it.
ARGUMENT:: rate
replies a second, per orbit.
ARGUMENT:: peakLag
how long a peak is held, in seconds.

METHOD:: stopMeters
Switch the replies off again.

METHOD:: setFader
METHOD:: setMute
METHOD:: setSolo
The mixer. Each sets its state for orbit code::i:: and applies the result to
every orbit whose level changed: the level is written into the orbit's
code::defaultParentEvent:: as code::orbitAmp:: and pushed to the fader effect
through its change tracking, so the synth and every event that follows agree.
Ends with code::changed(\mixer)::, which the window follows.
ARGUMENT:: i
the orbit index, 0 for d1.
ARGUMENT:: amp
the level, 0 to 4, for code::setFader::.
ARGUMENT:: bool
on or off, for code::setMute:: and code::setSolo::.

METHOD:: effectiveAmp
What the fader effect of orbit code::i:: is told: 0 when muted or when another
orbit is soloed, the fader's value otherwise.

METHOD:: faderValues
METHOD:: muteStates
METHOD:: soloStates
The mixer's state, one entry per orbit. nil until the orbits exist.

METHOD:: applyAmps
Push the state onto the orbits; the three setters call it.

METHOD:: setOrbitParam
Set a key of the orbit's plumbing - code::\limitertype:: - the same way: into
the default event, then to the running synth.

METHOD:: wakeOrbit
Push the orbit's default event to every always-on effect of it: the fader takes
its level and the monitor is resumed if it was asleep. SuperDirt's effects pause
themselves after four seconds of silence and only a set resumes them, which is
what every event does on its way in; a preview from the sample editor does the
same through this.
ARGUMENT:: orbit
a link::Classes/DirtOrbit::.

METHOD:: releaseEffect
link::Classes/GlobalDirtEffect#-release::, made to free a sleeping synth too.
code::DirtPause:: puts a quiet effect to sleep with a code::DetectSilence::, and
a synth it has put to sleep pauses itself again in the first block after an
code::/n_run:: that does not come with its code::dirt_resumed:: trigger.
code::release:: sends none, so a sleeping synth never finishes its release and
stays in the node tree, paused, holding its memory, until the next cmd-period -
measured: every effect that had run and gone quiet was left behind by each
reload. This sends the trigger with the gate. Everything here that takes an
effect out or starts it again goes through this or link::#-rebuildOrbit::.
ARGUMENT:: fx
a link::Classes/GlobalDirtEffect::.
ARGUMENT:: releaseTime
seconds, 0.2 as in code::release::.

METHOD:: tracksOf
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
the orbit's number.
RETURNS:: its link::Classes/SDGTrack::s, one per global effect, in chain order.

METHOD:: trackOf
ARGUMENT:: fx
a link::Classes/GlobalDirtEffect::.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGTrack:: it is the effect of, or nil.

METHOD:: effectParams
The keys a global effect listens to: what its file registered, or else its
SynthDef's controls without the plumbing.
ARGUMENT:: name
the effect's name.
RETURNS:: an Array of Symbols, or nil when there is no such SynthDef.

METHOD:: addTrack
A new track with the named global effect. Nothing else in the chain restarts.
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
the orbit's number.
ARGUMENT:: name
the effect's name, without the channel count.
ARGUMENT:: index
its position among the tracks, the end by default.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGTrack::, or nil with a warning when there is no such SynthDef or the orbit has it already.

METHOD:: setTrackEffect
Another global effect on a track. The old one is released with its tail; the
new one starts between the same inserts, at the same fader.
ARGUMENT:: track
an link::Classes/SDGTrack::.
ARGUMENT:: name
the effect's name, without the channel count.
RETURNS:: the new link::Classes/GlobalDirtEffect::, or nil with a warning when there is no such SynthDef or the orbit has it already.

METHOD:: moveTrack
A track to another position among its orbit's tracks. Its synths are moved, not
started again.
ARGUMENT:: track
an link::Classes/SDGTrack::.
ARGUMENT:: toIndex
its new position.

METHOD:: removeTrack
A track and its effect out of the orbit, its inserts with them.
ARGUMENT:: track
an link::Classes/SDGTrack::.

METHOD:: addTrackInsert
An insert on a track, as link::#-addInsert:: does on an orbit.
ARGUMENT:: track
an link::Classes/SDGTrack::.
ARGUMENT:: place
code::\pre::, before its effect, or code::\post::, after it.
ARGUMENT:: kind
code::\eq::, code::\compressor::, code::\insight:: or code::\vst::.
ARGUMENT:: index
its position among the inserts of that place, the end by default.
ARGUMENT:: plugin
a code::\vst:: insert's plugin.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGInsert::, or nil.

METHOD:: insertListOf
ARGUMENT:: insert
an link::Classes/SDGInsert::.
RETURNS:: the list it is in, its track's or its orbit's.

METHOD:: adoptEffects
A track for each effect that reached the orbit's list from code without one.
ARGUMENT:: orbit
a link::Classes/DirtOrbit::.

METHOD:: silentBus
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/Bus:: a track's inserts read as their effect bus; nothing writes to it.

METHOD:: rebuildOrbit
link::Classes/DirtOrbit#-initNodeTree:: - every effect of the orbit started
again, in the order of its code::globalEffects:: - after link::#-releaseEffect::
on each, since the stock rebuild leaves the sleeping ones behind.
ARGUMENT:: orbit
a link::Classes/DirtOrbit::.

METHOD:: returns
RETURNS:: the return tracks (link::Classes/SDGReturn::), by letter.

METHOD:: returnAt
ARGUMENT:: letter
its letter, as a Symbol or String.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGReturn::, or nil.

METHOD:: addReturn
A return track with a send from every orbit, and to and from every return,
all at nothing. Nothing that plays restarts. Its effects are inserts: link::#-addBusInsert::.
ARGUMENT:: letter
its letter; the next one free by default.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGReturn::, or nil with a warning when there are no orbits or the letter is taken.

METHOD:: removeReturn
A return out, with its inserts and every send to it and from it.
ARGUMENT:: ret
the link::Classes/SDGReturn::.

METHOD:: addBusInsert
An insert at a position in the one chain of a return or the master.
ARGUMENT:: track
the link::Classes/SDGBusTrack::.
ARGUMENT:: kind
code::\eq::, code::\compressor::, code::\insight::, code::\vst:: or an sc fx kit kind.
ARGUMENT:: index
its position; the end by default.
ARGUMENT:: plugin
a VST insert's plugin: a name or a file path.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGInsert::, or nil.

METHOD:: master
RETURNS:: the master track (link::Classes/SDGMaster::), nil when link::#*useMaster:: is false.

METHOD:: addMaster
The master, if there is none: everything that goes code::\master:: writes to it from now on.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGMaster::.

METHOD:: removeMaster
The master out, with its inserts: what went to it writes to the hardware again.

METHOD:: busTracks
RETURNS:: every return and the master, in the order they run.

METHOD:: copyInserts
Keep inserts, with every setting - a plugin with its state, read back a moment later -
to paste on any orbit, effect track, return or the master.
ARGUMENT:: list
an Array of link::Classes/SDGInsert::s.
RETURNS:: what was kept, as their sessionData.

METHOD:: pasteInserts
What link::#-copyInserts:: kept, as new inserts.
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
an orbit's index, or nil with a track.
ARGUMENT:: track
an link::Classes/SDGTrack:: or link::Classes/SDGBusTrack::, or nil.
ARGUMENT:: place
on an effect track, code::\pre:: or code::\post::.
ARGUMENT:: index
where the first goes; the end by default.
RETURNS:: the new link::Classes/SDGInsert::s.

METHOD:: insertClipboard
RETURNS:: what link::#-copyInserts:: kept, nil before.

METHOD:: copyTrack
Keep an orbit's effect track: its effect, the parameters set on the orbit, its level and inserts.
ARGUMENT:: track
the link::Classes/SDGTrack::.

METHOD:: pasteTrack
What link::#-copyTrack:: kept, as a new track of an orbit.
ARGUMENT:: orbitIndex
the orbit's index.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGTrack::, or nil when nothing was copied or the orbit has that effect already.

METHOD:: trackClipboard
RETURNS:: what link::#-copyTrack:: kept, nil before.

METHOD:: setSend
An orbit's send to a return.
ARGUMENT:: i
the orbit's number.
ARGUMENT:: ret
the link::Classes/SDGReturn::, or its letter.
ARGUMENT:: amp
the level, 0 to 1.

METHOD:: sendOf
ARGUMENT:: i
the orbit's number.
ARGUMENT:: ret
the link::Classes/SDGReturn::, or its letter.
RETURNS:: the level of that send, 0 to 1.

METHOD:: setReturnSend
One return's send to another, or to itself. It is only heard while link::#-returnFeedback:: is on.
ARGUMENT:: from
the link::Classes/SDGReturn:: it comes from, or its letter.
ARGUMENT:: to
the one it goes to, or its letter.
ARGUMENT:: amp
the level, 0 to 1.

METHOD:: returnFeedback
Whether returns may send to returns. Off by default. See link::Classes/SDGReturn#Feedback::.
ARGUMENT:: bool
true or false.

METHOD:: returnGroup
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/Group:: every return and the master run in, right after SuperDirt's; nil before the first.

METHOD:: returnHeads
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/Group:: at its head where every return's feedback is read, before any return writes.

METHOD:: masterGroup
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/Group:: at its tail where the master runs.

METHOD:: orderBusses
Walk the nodes of every return and the master into the order of their chains. Nothing restarts.

METHOD:: orderReturns
The same as link::#-orderBusses::.

METHOD:: loadSamples
Append a folder to the sound library: one bank per subfolder, files numbered by
name, the way Dirt-Samples is laid out, and the sound files lying in the folder
itself as a bank named after it (link::#*bankName::). Banks already loaded stay,
and a file a bank already holds is not read again, so a folder can be loaded
again for what is new in it. One level down only.
ARGUMENT:: folderPath
a String; code::~:: is expanded.
ARGUMENT:: warnEmpty
whether a folder with nothing new is warned about. true by default.
RETURNS:: the names of the banks that got files, an Array of Symbols; empty when
there was nothing new.

METHOD:: addSample
Append one file to a bank as its last index, or make the bank if the name is
new.
ARGUMENT:: filePath
a String; code::~:: is expanded.
ARGUMENT:: bankName
the bank, a Symbol.

METHOD:: reloadSample
Read a sample that was rewritten on disk back into the same link::Classes/Buffer::,
so every event keeps its bufnum and Tidal plays the new file under the same
name and number. The header is read first, so the event's frame count is right
before the server has read the file. The sample editor calls this after a save.
ARGUMENT:: name
the bank.
ARGUMENT:: index
the sample in it.
RETURNS:: the Buffer, or nil when there is none at that name and index or the
file is gone.

METHOD:: bufferAt
The library's Buffer for a bank and index, wrapping the index the way Tidal
does, or nil.

METHOD:: gui
Open the control window, or bring it to the front. Needs the orbits, so after
code::~dirt.start::.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGWindow::, or nil when there are no orbits yet.

METHOD:: window
The link::Classes/Window:: while the control window is open, nil otherwise.

METHOD:: sync
The link::Classes/SDGSync:: of the library, made when first asked for: longer samples on one
tempo and in one key, written as Tidal code. code::free:: stops its preview and closes its window.

METHOD:: tags
The link::Classes/SDGSampleTags:: of the library, made when first asked for.
Its files are shared by every loader: they live in
link::Classes/SDGSampleTags#*folder::.

METHOD:: editorMaxMinutes
The longest file the sample editor opens, in minutes of stereo at 44.1 kHz
(the editor counts frames times channels, so a mono file may be twice as
long): 4.5 by default, 1 to 100, in half minutes. Set in the settings window
too, kept on this computer for the next run, put into
code::SampleEdit.maxSamples:: whenever it changes and when the loader starts.
Notifies code::\editorMaxMinutes::.

Opening a file reads all of it in the language, about 1 s a minute of stereo,
and sclang does nothing else meanwhile: SuperDirt plays nothing while a long
file opens. Past about 101 minutes sclang crashes on the one Signal the file
is read into, so the editor refuses those whatever this says
(code::SampleEdit.hardLimit::).

METHOD:: warnOutOfOrbit
Whether SuperDirt posts code::event falls out of existing orbits:: for every
event sent to an orbit it does not have (code::# orbit 8:: with eight orbits).
This is SuperDirt's own code::warnOutOfOrbit::, true by default, as in
SuperDirt, set in the settings window too (strong::post window::), kept on
this computer and set when the loader starts. link::#-free:: puts back what
SuperDirt had. Notifies code::\warnOutOfOrbit::.

METHOD:: edit
Open the sample editor on one sample of the library.
ARGUMENT:: name
the bank.
ARGUMENT:: index
the sample in it.
ARGUMENT:: orbit
the orbit index the preview plays through.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/SDGSampleEditor::.

METHOD:: free
Take our modules, players and global effects out of code::~dirt::, forget the
names, put the orbits' code::orbitAmp:: back to nothing and close the window.
Synths stay on the server.

METHOD:: dirt
The link::Classes/SuperDirt:: this loads into.

METHOD:: path
The repository folder, found from the class file. Works through the
extensions symlink and for a quark install alike.

METHOD:: synthDir
METHOD:: moduleDir
METHOD:: playerDir
METHOD:: effectDir
The four folders.

METHOD:: dirFor
The folder of a kind, one of link::#*kinds::.

METHOD:: verbose
Whether to post one line per file loaded. true by default.


EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;

(
s.waitForBoot {
	~dirt = SuperDirt(2, s);
	~dirt.loadSoundFiles;
	s.sync;
	~dirt.start(57120, 0 ! 12);
	s.sync;
	~sdg = SuperDirtGUI(~dirt);
};
);

~sdg.synthNames;           // -> [ sdgsine, sdgacid, sdgchoir, ... ]
~sdg.moduleNames;          // -> [ sdgcomb ]
~sdg.samplePlayerNames;    // -> [ sdgwarp ]
~sdg.globalEffectNames;    // -> [ global_sdgspace ]
~sdg.gui;

// from tidal:
//   d1 $ s "sdgsine" # n "0 7 12"
//   d1 $ s "bd*4" # pF "sdgcomb" 0.4
//   d1 $ s "breaks152" # pF "sdgwarp" 1 # speed 0.5 # pF "sdgpitch" 1.5
//   d1 $ s "bd*4" # pF "sdgspace" 0.5

// the same events from sclang, without tidal
~dirt.orbits[0].valuePairs([\s, \sdgsine, \n, 7, \sdgcomb, 0.4, \sdgspace, 0.5]);
~dirt.orbits[0].valuePairs([\s, \breaks152, \n, 0, \sdgwarp, 1, \speed, 0.5]);

// the mixer from code; the window follows
~sdg.setFader(0, 0.5);
~sdg.setMute(0, true);
~sdg.setMute(0, false);

// the sample editor on one sample
~sdg.edit(\bd, 3);

// a player of your own: players/mywarp.scd, from the example, loaded
~sdg.newFile(\players, \mywarp);
~sdg.sourceFile(\players, \mywarp);

// edited it? read that file again - the window does this when you save
~sdg.loadFile("players/mywarp.scd");

// or every file in synths/, modules/, players/ and effects/
~sdg.reload;

// done
~sdg.free;
::

There is a startup you can evaluate a block at a time in
code::examples/superdirt-gui-demo.scd:: in the repository.
